{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll test v3 against the saved failures and the frozen gold. The key question is whether code can derive each checked relationship from the supplied material, without relying on a label the answer gave itself."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"     155 docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md\n     1\t# judging-v13: related number questions judged together, with a checked calculation (design v3, for council round 46c)\n     2\t\n     3\tOpened by Alex (D726) for the bounded test of D725. Harness-only until a later decision: no product configuration names it, and\n     4\tthe ordinary launchers refuse it (D359, D723). The frozen judge (judging-v7, poc-v118) is unchanged.\n     5\t\n     6\tv2 answered round 46 (`codex-r46.md`, changes marked [46.n]). v3 answers round 46b (`codex-r46b.md`, marked [46b.n]). Rounds 46 and\n     7\t46b found the same weakness five times: the checks trusted what the answer declares about itself (its `uses` list, its labels, a\n     8\tshortened span). **v3's structural rule:** code derives every relation it checks from the calculation itself and from the passage\n     9\ttext, and the answer declares nothing code relies on without checking it:\n    10\t- each outcome's inputs are found by walking the figure graph;\n    11\t- a future quantity shared by the family enters any forecast only through the family's one forecast node for it;\n    12\t- a source figure must equal a complete numeric expression that code extracts from the passage.\n    13\t\n    14\t## 1. What changes\n    15\t\n    16\t| Piece | judging-v7 (today) | judging-v13 |\n    17\t|---|---|---|\n    18\t| Unit | one question per call | one **family** per call: the number questions of one company whose quantities overlap (a total and its segment or product line, a level and its growth, and a later year declared to rest on an earlier year's base [46.2]). Each keeps its own answer |\n    19\t| Who forms a family | none (0 dependencies) | **declared** for the test from the stored cases, with typed relations: `part_of(part, whole)` and `same_base(later, earlier)`. Production would need a matching version that names them |\n    20\t| Evidence shown | each question's own evidence in view (D198) | the union of the members' evidence in view, each passage once with its attribution; themes once |\n    21\t| Previous conclusion | rung and reasons, cut at 800 characters, unmarked | each member's previous rung and outcome, labelled \"our earlier forecast, not a source\". It can enter a calculation only as a `prior_forecast` figure (§2), never as a source or a market reference |\n    22\t| Answer | prose fields plus one answer-wide citation list | a calculation (figures) plus exactly one answer per member (§2) |\n    23\t| Request text | judging-v7 | a full v13 request written and audited (§5) [46.6] |\n    24\t| Failure | refused twice: the question is left as it was | refused twice: the family is recorded **unresolved** and no member is updated. A member may also answer `unresolved` itself (§2) [46.5] |\n    25\t\n    26\t## 2. The answer format (closed set; the schema enforces shape, code enforces §3)\n    27\t\n    28\t```\n    29\tfigures: [ { id,\n    30\t             what: { population, measure, scope, period },     # canonical: measure is the quantity (\"revenue\"), not its basis [46.1]\n    31\t             basis: \"reported\" | \"guidance\" | \"consensus\" | \"third_party\" | \"forecast\" | \"prior_forecast\",\n    32\t             scenario: \"base\" | \"<named>\",                       # default \"base\" [46.1]\n    33\t             kind: \"source\" | \"computed\" | \"estimate\",\n    34\t             value | {low, high} | {bound: \">\" | \">=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\" | \"~\", value},  unit,\n    35\t             # kind source:   evidence: \"<id>\", expression: <index of a complete expression code extracts from that passage>,\n    36\t             #                component: \"midpoint\" | \"low\" | \"high\" | \"bound\" | \"multiplier\" | \"value\"            [46b.2]\n    37\t             # kind computed: inputs: [...], operation: sum|difference|product|ratio|growth|apply_growth|share|range_from_relative,\n    38\t             #                relation (sum only): \"accounting_parts\" | \"time_aggregation\" | \"stated_in_addition\" | \"overlap_unknown\",\n    39\t             #                disputed (overlap_unknown only): \"<the operand whose inclusion is in doubt>\"            [46b.4]\n    40\t             #                bridge (when inputs' what differ from the output's): \"<figure id of an estimate>\"   [46.3]\n    41\t             # kind estimate: basis_text, rests_on: [figure ids, evidence ids],\n    42\t             #                adjusts (optional): { figure: \"<id>\", type: \"discount\" | \"uplift\" | \"trim\" }            [46.3]\n    43\t             #                a bridge estimate also names `converts: [operand ids]` and the assumption           [46b.3]\n    44\t           } ]\n    45\tanswers: [ { view_id, shape: \"forecast\", outcome: \"<figure id>\", rung,                 # [46b.5] two shapes\n    46\t             reference: { figure: \"<id>\" } | { unavailable: \"<why>\" },                # per answer [46.2]\n    47\t             reasons, cites, pushes, theme_pushes, changed, why_changed }\n    48\t         | { view_id, shape: \"unresolved\", why, cites, changed, why_changed } ]       # no outcome, no rung\n    49\t```\n    50\t\n    51\t- **An arithmetic intermediate and the analyst's final range are distinct figures** [46.2]. For example, q02's $392B × 92% is a\n    52\t  `computed` figure. The final $370–400B is an `estimate` resting on it and on q01's range. Code checks the intermediate's arithmetic\n    53\t  and never requires the final range to equal it.\n    54\t- **`prior_forecast`** is a figure the system forecast earlier. It needs an exact pointer: the view, day and figure of an earlier\n    55\t  answer shown in the request. Within one family call, a declared `same_base` reads the family's own forecast node of the earlier\n    56\t  period at the same snapshot (q22 reads q01's) [46b.3].\n    57\t- **Scenarios carry ranges** [46b.1]. A forecast node is a point within a scenario. A range across members is written as paired\n    58\t  scenarios (for example `low`, `high`) whose nodes stay paired. The analyst's final answer may be a range `estimate` resting on the\n    59\t  scenario outcomes.\n    60\t- **The family declaration** (by the harness, not by the answer) lists the shared quantities and periods the members depend on, for\n    61\t  example company revenue for each FY2027 quarter, and the typed relations [46b.1].\n    62\t\n    63\t## 3. What code checks (identity and arithmetic only)\n    64\t\n    65\t| Check | Rule | Refuses |\n    66\t|---|---|---|\n    67\t| **C1 source expression** [46b.2] | code extracts every complete numeric expression from the cited passage as shown, with its qualifiers and unit. Points: \"$108.0 billion\". Ranges: \"$105–110 billion\". Relative: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" is midpoint 108 ±2%. Bounds: \"more than $730 billion\" (>730), \"at least\" (>=). Approximate bounds: \"nearly $800 billion\" (approx_below 800). Multipliers: \"double\" ×2, \"more than double\" (>×2). A source figure names one extracted expression and a component of it (midpoint, low, high, bound, multiplier), and must equal it exactly: value, unit, sign and qualifier. Any other wording cannot be a source figure | a figure that strips a qualifier ($730 from \"more than $730 billion\"); a bound used as a point; a wrong number within 0.5% (108.5); an unextracted span |\n    68\t| **C2 arithmetic** | a `computed` figure recomputes from its inputs by its operation. Rounding: to the figure's stated precision, at most 0.5% or 0.1 unit. `range_from_relative` makes 108 ± 2% into 105.84–110.16 | a wrong sum, share or endpoint |\n    69\t| **C3 part within whole** [46b.1] | for each declared `part_of`, in each scenario, for each declared period: the part's value, found by walking the graph from the part member's outcome to its node for that period, is at most the whole's. Annual and quarters are compared where the declaration names them | a segment quarter above the company quarter |\n    70\t| **C4 one forecast per shared quantity** [46b.1] | for each shared quantity and period the declaration names, each scenario has exactly one `basis: forecast` node. Any figure feeding a member's outcome (found by walking the graph) that measures that quantity and period must be that node or lie upstream of it. A guide, consensus or source figure of a future period may feed the forecast node, but cannot feed another member's calculation directly | v9 computing Data Center Q3 from the $108B guide while v14's company Q3 forecast is $113.5B, with the guide correctly labelled |\n    71\t| **C5 reference** [46b.3] | each answer's reference is `unavailable` with a reason, or a figure whose graph has at least one leaf that is a source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party from a passage whose speaker is not the system. The other leaves may be reported figures (a mix, a base year). No leaf may be `forecast` or `prior_forecast`. Its provenance is shown | the system's own forecast presented as the market's |\n    72\t| **C6 rung and outcome** [46.2] | the chosen rung's band overlaps the outcome (a point: contains it). The reasons say why that band, where the range overlaps several | a rung no part of the outcome falls in |\n    73\t| **C7 structure** [46.5] | exactly one answer per member; an answer's outcome `what` matches its member's question (population, measure, scope, period, from the question's typed key); figure ids are unique; references are not dangling and not circular; operations come from the closed set | a missing or doubled member, a borrowed outcome, a dangling or circular figure |\n    74\t| **C8 operand compatibility** [46b.3] | by operation. **sum/difference:** same measure, population and scope, with periods either disjoint and together forming the output period (`time_aggregation`), or identical with a declared relation (`accounting_parts` needs a declared `part_of`). **share:** numerator and denominator stated, with a declared `part_of` or a reported mix. **product/ratio:** a change of measure, population, scope or period not covered above needs a bridge estimate whose `converts` names the actual operands, and an assumption | 730/800 × $1.3T with no bridge naming e1786's four-company 2026 spending and e2535's five-company capex; a bridge that converts other operands |\n    75\t\n    76\t**Recorded, not refused** (visible to readers and to the page) [46.3]:\n    77\t- **Overlap:** a `sum` with relation `overlap_unknown`. Its alternate omits the named `disputed` operand, all else fixed [46b.4].\n    78\t- **Adjustment:** an `estimate` that `adjusts` a figure. Its alternate is the adjusted figure (R4: the bound \">40\").\n    79\t- **Bridge:** every bridge; alternate undetermined.\n    80\t- **Boundaries:** code recomputes, from unrounded values, each outcome under each defined alternate. It records which rung boundary the\n    81\t  assumption definitely or possibly decides: \"crosses $40B\" when the alternate is a bound, a rung change when the alternate is a\n    82\t  point. Where a downstream estimate has no formula, or no alternate is defined, it records \"undetermined\". A reader decides whether a\n    83\t  boundary-deciding assumption is supported. D721/D722: unresolved inclusion is never treated as proof of double counting.\n    84\t\n    85\t**The model keeps:** drivers, weights, discounts, scenarios, the choice among overlapping bands, and whether to step off the\n    86\treference. Code never prefers guidance, prescribes a haircut, refuses an estimate for being one, or picks a band from a midpoint.\n    87\t\n    88\t## 4. Tables\n    89\t\n    90\t**Value × reader.**\n    91\t- **The calculation, the recorded assumptions and band-deciding flags:** read by the checks, the readers' packets, and later the\n    92\t  page.\n    93\t- **`unresolved`:** read by the round (no member updated) and the bundle (\"Forecast unavailable\", D726).\n    94\t- **The family declaration:** read by the request builder, C3, C4 and C7.\n    95\t- **Nothing in poc-v118 reads any of it.**\n    96\t\n    97\t**New path × old checks.**\n    98\t- v7's id, push and theme checks apply per member answer. Rung checks apply to `forecast` answers only [46b.5]. `cites` names shown\n    99\t  evidence only.\n   100\t- `cites_views` is always empty.\n   101\t- v7's request bytes are unchanged (the frozen pins).\n   102\t\n   103\t**State × event (the test harness).** Inputs are fixed: the stored requests' passages and the gold packet's items, as of the stored\n   104\tday.\n   105\t\n   106\t| Event | Outcome |\n   107\t|---|---|\n   108\t| success | the calculation and answers are recorded with their check results |\n   109\t| refusal | the errors are recorded; one retry with them |\n   110\t| second refusal | the family is unresolved, recorded |\n   111\t| a member's own `unresolved` [46b.5] | recorded; the family is held (no member updated) when any other member's outcome depends on a shared quantity or relation involving it; otherwise the others publish if they pass |\n   112\t| crash midway | the attempt is recorded incomplete and counts as no result |\n   113\t\n   114\tNothing writes a product store. Production lifecycle (members closing or opening, families changing) is designed only if the test\n   115\tpasses.\n   116\t\n   117\t**Prior decisions:**\n   118\t- D24, D28: bounded to the figures open questions consume.\n   119\t- D198: evidence in view, unioned.\n   120\t- D260, D263: the standard kept.\n   121\t- D359, D723: no product launcher.\n   122\t- D695: number questions.\n   123\t- D711, D721, D722: linking passed conclusions, and unresolved inclusion is not double counting.\n   124\t- D725, D726.\n   125\t\n   126\t## 5. The v13 request text (written and audited at $0 before the gate closes) [46.6]\n   127\t\n   128\t- **The basis:** judging-v7's standard sentences are kept, among them the reference, run-rate, discount, sizing, identities and\n   129\t  consistency.\n   130\t- **Replaced where they conflict with a family:**\n   131\t  - the single-question delivery (\"the current conclusions of dependencies\");\n   132\t  - \"the previous conclusion carries\" pieces not shown, which becomes \"our earlier forecast, not a source\";\n   133\t  - the one-reference, one-outcome output sentence;\n   134\t  - the dependency-consistency sentence, which becomes the family's own consistency, the same clause made family-wide.\n   135\t- **Added:** how to write figures (kinds, bases, spans, relations, bridges, `adjusts`, `unresolved`).\n   136\t- **The audit:** every changed sentence is shown with its source (D260, D263, D725, this design), read against a stored family\n   137\t  request, and its character count before and after is reported (AGENTS §6).\n   138\t\n   139\t## 6. The $0 gate (registered; each defect must produce its named finding) [46.5]\n   140\t\n   141\t| Item | Content | Pass: the named finding |\n   142\t|---|---|---|\n   143\t| R1a | old-2 8729 (v14) and 8737 (v9) on 09-20, as stored: v9 Q4 146, v14 Q4 127 | **C3** on Q4 |\n   144\t| R1b | the same, with Q3 as stored: v9's Data Center Q3 (99.9) computed from the guide midpoint 108 (labelled `guidance`, correctly) × 92.5%, v14's company Q3 forecast 113.5. Also a variant where v9 lists nothing extra but its graph still reads the guide | **C4** on Q3 company revenue, independent of C3, in both variants |\n   145\t| R1c (control) | a guide node (108 ± 2%, from e2499's span) beside one shared Q3 forecast node (113.5) used by both | passes |\n   146\t| R2 | old-2 8734: $25B (e2620) + $18B (e2889) written as `overlap_unknown` with `disputed` the $18B, + MediaTek, + talks as estimates | recorded overlap; alternate about $35B decides the $50B boundary (conditional) |\n   147\t| R3 | old-2 8595: 730 (e1786, \">730\") / 800 (e2535, approx_below 800) × 1,300 | **C1** when the qualifiers are stripped; with them kept, **C8** without a bridge naming those operands; with a bridge: recorded, undetermined |\n   148\t| R4 | old-3 9166: 20 × (>2) (e3660, \"more than double\") gives >40, trimmed to 38–39 by `adjusts: trim` | recorded adjustment that definitely crosses the $40B boundary (rung 2 is $40–50B, rung 1 $50B+) |\n   149\t| G1–G4 (controls) | q01, q02, q19, q22, from `gold-v1.json`'s reasoning (expressions from the packet items). q01's Q1 + Q2 as `time_aggregation`; q02's comparison $392B (consensus) × 92% (reported mix); q22 in one family with q01, reading q01's FY2027 node | all pass, estimates visible; q02's reference passes C5; q19's overlapping range passes C6 with its reason |\n   150\t| P1–P3 (plants, on control copies) | an amount absent from its span; a wrong sum; a prior forecast as the reference | P1 **C1**, P2 **C2**, P3 **C5**, each for its intended reason |\n   151\t| T1–T7 (C1 forms, real passages) | e2499 midpoint 108 ±2% (accepted as `midpoint`); e1786 \">730\"; e2535 approx_below 800 and the point 1,300; e3660 \">×2\"; stripped forms \"$730\", \"800\", \"double\" against those passages; 108.5 against e2499 | T1–T3 parse as stated; the stripped forms and 108.5 refused |\n   152\t| U1–U2 (paths) | a member answering `unresolved` while another member's outcome needs its shared quantity; a calculation refused twice | the family held, recorded; the family unresolved, recorded |\n   153\t\n   154\t- **Stop:** any missing finding, a finding for the wrong reason, or a control the format cannot express.\n   155\t- **Then:** Council B on the gate's output. The paid gate's ceiling goes to Alex.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '52,72p;78,94p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md; rg -n 'q01|q02|q19|q22' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | head -15; sed -n '92,120p' AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '52,72p;78,94p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md; rg -n 'q01|q02|q19|q22' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | head -15; sed -n '92,120p' AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"## The converged design (for a new judge version Alex opens)\n\n- **One judging call per related family.** Same company and fiscal year, overlapping quantities. Each question keeps its own answer,\n  and every answer reads from one calculation.\n- **The judge labels the figures it uses:** what each measures (population, scope, period) and whether it is a source figure, a\n  calculation or an assumption. Extraction v74i is unchanged.\n- **Code checks identity and arithmetic only:**\n  1. a source figure appears in the shown passage it cites;\n  2. the arithmetic recomputes;\n  3. a declared part fits within its whole for the same period and scenario;\n  4. one value per shared quantity, role and scenario across the family;\n  5. a market reference points to shown external material, or says none.\n\n  The model keeps importance: drivers, weights, discounts, and overlap stated as an assumption. A blind reader judges whether an\n  assumption that crosses a boundary is supported.\n- **When figures cannot be reconciled, the forecast shows as unresolved,** not as two incompatible answers.\n- **Bounded to the figures the open questions consume** (D24, D28).\n- **Families:** declared from stored cases for the test. Production would need a matching version that names the relationship.\n- **The frozen gold's reasoning is kept:** q01 (upside drivers without a fixed increment), q02 (a segment from the shared total and a\n  sourced mix), q19 (an execution discount), q22 (FY2028 from q01's base, widened by judgement).\n\n## The one test (registered with the design)\n\n- **The $0 gate.** Hand-reconstruct, in the new format:\n  - old-2 8729/8737, 8734 and 8595, and old-3 9166;\n  - the four gold controls;\n  - three planted errors (an amount absent from its quote, a wrong sum, an internal forecast labelled consensus).\n\n  The format must expose every defect, represent every control, and fail every plant. Any miss stops the line.\n- **The paid gate** (Alex's ceiling; 36 calls, about $0.2–0.4 estimated):\n  - **The arms:** the 09-20 total and Data Center pair in each draw, three samples each, judged jointly and separately under the same\n    format and checks. Plus three samples each on the O, S and E requests. Every output is read against its source with the arm\n    hidden.\n  - **Pass:** the joint arm publishes both answers usable and source-supported in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot,\n    and at least 3 more usable pairs than the separate arm. Zero published hard contradictions, internal-as-market references, or\n    unresolved overlap, scope or unsized adjustments that decide the band.\n  - **Stop:** any such published defect, either coverage line missed, or an incomplete comparison.\n26:    \"q01\",\n27:    \"q02\",\n34:    \"q19\",\n36:    \"q22\",\n51:   \"question_id\": \"q01\",\n101:   \"question_id\": \"q02\",\n117:    \"text\": \"Q1 Data Center $75.25bn (i129, i151); Q2 total guided $91bn (i281) at the same share is about $84bn, so the first half is roughly $159bn. My total-revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) at a 92-93% share, with SemiAnalysis expecting second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus (i106, i208), gives $370-400bn.\",\n553:    \"text\": \"Q1 non-GAAP EPS was $1.87 on $81.6bn of revenue (i129); Q2 guidance of $91bn at the same 75% gross margin (i281) gives about $2.09, so consensus needs about $4.73 in the second half. My revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) converted at that second-half rate gives $8.9-9.8, and the $80bn buyback (i301), after $19.3bn bought in Q1 (i131), trims the share count. I bet $9.0-10.0.\",\n581:   \"reasoning\": \"Consensus is $8.69, up 90.2% (i278); an earlier figure was $8.97 (i082). Q1 non-GAAP EPS was $1.87 on $81.6bn of revenue (i129), and Q2 guidance of $91bn at the same 75% gross margin (i281) gives about $2.09, so the first half is about $3.96 and consensus needs about $4.73 in the second half. My revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) converted at that second-half rate gives $8.9-9.8, and the $80bn buyback authorisation (i301) after $19.3bn bought in Q1 (i131) trims the share count. I bet $9.0-10.0, above consensus, with the upside case needing the operating leverage of q10. The risks are memory cost on the Rubin ramp (i217, i252) and the rumoured Vera Rubin lineup delays (i133).\"\n852:   \"question_id\": \"q19\",\n918:    \"text\": \"On my $400-435bn total (q01: i295, i106, i219), Data Center at 92% (i129) is $370-400bn; compute at about 80% of that (i151) is $295-320bn, and rack-level content on NVL72 systems (i216) lifts the product line toward $350bn; Hopper's residual is small, with old GPUs rented rather than bought (i289) and China at zero (i156). I bet $290-350bn.\",\n947:   \"reasoning\": \"No consensus for the line is stated. The Street's $392bn total (i037) at the Q1 Data Center share of 92% (i129), less networking at about a fifth of Data Center (i151), implies roughly $290-320bn of compute, nearly all of it Blackwell and Rubin; Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales from March 2026 to the end of 2027 (i079; 'orders' in i036) spans two fiscal years and exceeds consensus total revenue for that window, so it is a bookings claim, not a FY2027 sales figure. On my $400-435bn total (q01: i295, i106, i219), Data Center at 92% (i129) is $370-400bn, compute at about 80% (i151) is $295-320bn, and rack-level content on NVL72 systems (i216) lifts the product line toward $350bn; Hopper's residual is small, with old GPUs rented rather than bought (i289) and China at zero (i156). I bet $290-350bn, in line with the implied reference and tilted up with q01. Huang's $1 trillion (i079) argues for more; the Vera Rubin lineup delay rumour (i133) argues for less.\"\n966:   \"question_id\": \"q22\",\n983:    \"text\": \"A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the Street's 41% (i090) is $565-615bn; NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step; Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window; Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216) and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: the Kyber rack may slip to 2028 (i100) with Oberon-Rubin racks filling the gap (i181), and memory stays tight until 2028 (i252). I bet $560-640bn.\",\n1013:   \"reasoning\": \"Consensus is $554bn (i037), the 41% growth analysts project for next year (i090, i174). A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the same 41% is $565-615bn. NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step, Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window, Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216), and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: SemiAnalysis has the Kyber rack slipping to 2028 with the four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled (i100, i179), which pushes the next product step out of FY2028 and leaves Oberon-Rubin racks to fill it (i181); memory stays tight until 2028 (i252); and Meta's CEO sees slower AI-agent progress (i292). I bet $560-640bn, above consensus, with low confidence eighteen months out.\"\n\nCode written before the thinking costs rounds of review. A design holds these tables before code and before any review; a blank cell\nmeans it is not ready:\n\n1. **State × event**, for work that spans more than one step, run or day: every state against every event it can meet (success,\n   refusal, a refused retry, an external failure, a crash midway, work still in flight when a run ends, a later run on a later date,\n   configuration changed in between, an input corrected while waiting, another writer of the same data, two scopes at once), including\n   the work before its first step and after its terminal outcome. Each cell names the transition, what is written, and the date every\n   read and write uses.\n2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and every input source the\n   value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to how often something fires is counted\n   against every reader of its output.\n3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   design and in the question to Alex.\n\n- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n  question, not something to build.\n- A foundation's acceptance carries a size and speed budget from its first control, not only correctness.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib, sqlite3, re\nfrom decimal import Decimal as D\np=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\'').resolve()\nc=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nfor call,eids in [(8729,('\\\\''e2499'\\\\'','\\\\''e4762'\\\\'')),(8737,('\\\\''e3219'\\\\'',)),(8734,('\\\\''e2620'\\\\'','\\\\''e2889'\\\\'')),(8595,('\\\\''e1786'\\\\'','\\\\''e2535'\\\\''))]:\n req,res=c.execute('\\\\''select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n t=json.loads(req)['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n print(call,json.loads(res)['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n for eid in eids: print(eid,re.search(r'\\\\''\"'^- '\"'\\\\''+eid+r'\\\\'' \\\\[.*\\\\n    Quote: (.*)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',t,re.M)[1])\nnodes={'\\\\''guide'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''guide_pct'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''beat'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''mix'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''high'\\\\'':('\\\\''guide'\\\\'','\\\\''guide_pct'\\\\''),\n       '\\\\''shared_forecast'\\\\'':('\\\\''high'\\\\'','\\\\''beat'\\\\''), '\\\\''bypass'\\\\'':('\\\\''guide'\\\\'','\\\\''mix'\\\\''),\n       '\\\\''compliant'\\\\'':('\\\\''shared_forecast'\\\\'','\\\\''mix'\\\\'')}\ndef ancestors(node):\n return {v for parent in nodes[node] for v in {parent}|ancestors(parent)}\nshared='\\\\''shared_forecast'\\\\''\nsame_q3_company={'\\\\''guide'\\\\'','\\\\''high'\\\\'',shared}\nfor path in ('\\\\''bypass'\\\\'','\\\\''compliant'\\\\''):\n found=ancestors(path)&same_q3_company\n print(path,'\\\\''company nodes'\\\\'',sorted(found),'\\\\''v3 C4 passes'\\\\'',found<={shared}|ancestors(shared),'\\\\''traverses chosen forecast'\\\\'',shared in ancestors(path))\nprint('\\\\''v9 Q3'\\\\'',D('\\\\''108'\\\\'')*D('\\\\''.925'\\\\''),'\\\\''v14 Q3 from the quote'\\\\'',D('\\\\''108'\\\\'')*D('\\\\''1.02'\\\\'')*D('\\\\''1.031'\\\\''))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib, sqlite3, re\nfrom decimal import Decimal as D\np=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\'').resolve()\nc=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nfor call,eids in [(8729,('\\\\''e2499'\\\\'','\\\\''e4762'\\\\'')),(8737,('\\\\''e3219'\\\\'',)),(8734,('\\\\''e2620'\\\\'','\\\\''e2889'\\\\'')),(8595,('\\\\''e1786'\\\\'','\\\\''e2535'\\\\''))]:\n req,res=c.execute('\\\\''select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n t=json.loads(req)['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n print(call,json.loads(res)['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n for eid in eids: print(eid,re.search(r'\\\\''\"'^- '\"'\\\\''+eid+r'\\\\'' \\\\[.*\\\\n    Quote: (.*)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',t,re.M)[1])\nnodes={'\\\\''guide'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''guide_pct'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''beat'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''mix'\\\\'':(), '\\\\''high'\\\\'':('\\\\''guide'\\\\'','\\\\''guide_pct'\\\\''),\n       '\\\\''shared_forecast'\\\\'':('\\\\''high'\\\\'','\\\\''beat'\\\\''), '\\\\''bypass'\\\\'':('\\\\''guide'\\\\'','\\\\''mix'\\\\''),\n       '\\\\''compliant'\\\\'':('\\\\''shared_forecast'\\\\'','\\\\''mix'\\\\'')}\ndef ancestors(node):\n return {v for parent in nodes[node] for v in {parent}|ancestors(parent)}\nshared='\\\\''shared_forecast'\\\\''\nsame_q3_company={'\\\\''guide'\\\\'','\\\\''high'\\\\'',shared}\nfor path in ('\\\\''bypass'\\\\'','\\\\''compliant'\\\\''):\n found=ancestors(path)&same_q3_company\n print(path,'\\\\''company nodes'\\\\'',sorted(found),'\\\\''v3 C4 passes'\\\\'',found<={shared}|ancestors(shared),'\\\\''traverses chosen forecast'\\\\'',shared in ancestors(path))\nprint('\\\\''v9 Q3'\\\\'',D('\\\\''108'\\\\'')*D('\\\\''.925'\\\\''),'\\\\''v14 Q3 from the quote'\\\\'',D('\\\\''108'\\\\'')*D('\\\\''1.02'\\\\'')*D('\\\\''1.031'\\\\''))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"8729 About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\ne2499 Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\ne4762 Nvidia has exceeded the high end of its revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters. Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.\n8737 Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp, with a discounted contribution from SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half Data Center revenue would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\ne3219 Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n8734 About $53 billion: $25 billion of commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, then the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure and a risk-adjusted contribution from the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions.\ne2620 These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\ne2889 The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n8595 About $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate $730 billion/$800 billion four-to-five share proxy to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five calendar-2027 CapEx estimate (e2535; repeated in e4756 and later reports).\ne1786 Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year, and they expect that number to increase next year.\ne2535 With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\nbypass company nodes ['guide'] v3 C4 passes True traverses chosen forecast False\ncompliant company nodes ['guide', 'high', 'shared_forecast'] v3 C4 passes True traverses chosen forecast True\nv9 Q3 99.900 v14 Q3 from the quote 113.57496\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"NOT READY\n\n**The remaining structural defect is that v3 checks which figures appear in the graph without checking how each figure reaches the published answer.** In **R1b**, the $108B guide feeds v14’s beat-adjusted $113.5B forecast. V9 uses that same $108B guide directly to calculate $99.9B for Data Center. I walked both paths in memory. C4 accepts both because the guide is upstream of the shared forecast, even on the path that bypasses it. The gate could appear to work if the fixture gives v9 a separate copy of the guide. That would test figure IDs rather than whether the cards share a calculation. [Design C4 and R1b](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:69), [Old-2 calls 8729 and 8737](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite)\n\n**The rule I’d accept is to check every path by which a number contributes to the outcome.** Within a scenario, every use of the shared Q3 company forecast must pass through the chosen forecast node. The guide can help calculate that node. It cannot reach either card as a substitute for it. Make R1b reuse the *same* guide node on both paths and require C4 to catch the bypass. Apply the same rule to market comparisons. C5 currently allows an arbitrary $414B estimate to pass if it points to a genuine outside figure somewhere underneath. Require a market comparison to be a direct external figure or a recomputable derivation from permitted external and reported inputs. That keeps q02’s consensus × reported mix valid. Exercise P3 with both a direct prior forecast and the same figure wrapped in an estimate citing outside evidence. [Design C4–C5 and P3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:69), [Frozen q02](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:101)\n\nTwo registered cases also need a valid representation before they can test their intended checks:\n\n- **R2 reaches a period error before the overlap check.** C8 allows identical periods or periods that partition the output. The $25B is a balance **as of July 26**. The $18B is a commitment **for the rest of the fiscal year**. Keep both source descriptions and show the disputed $18B explicitly as a *conditional increment* to the balance. Then dropping that increment can produce the registered conditional result of about $35B without changing either source’s period. [Design C8 and R2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:74)\n- **R3’s preserved qualifier has no allowed shape.** C1 and R3 require `approx_below 800` for **nearly $800B**, but the answer format does not admit it. Define that representation and require it to pass C1 before the no-bridge case reaches C8. Otherwise R3 can stop at the schema and never exercise the scope check. [Design figure format, C1 and R3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:28)\n\n**Wording notes**\n\n- R1c and P1 still say `span`. V3 identifies a source expression by index and component."}}
